WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Zero Trust is a security strategy, not a single product, feature, or vendor service.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.765
Technology enables the strategy,

00:00:06.765 --> 00:00:11.015
but governance and operating processes determine how access decisions support business and

00:00:11.015 --> 00:00:12.095
risk objectives.

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:14.480
Leadership establishes direction and accountability.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.930
Executives and risk owners define priorities, approve investment,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:22.145
resolve cross-team barriers, and decide how the organization balances security,

00:00:22.145 --> 00:00:24.520
user experience, cost, and mission needs.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.600
Governance translates strategy into policy.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.102
It defines protected resources, identity and device expectations,

00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:34.570
least privilege principles, monitoring requirements, exceptions, metrics, review cycles,

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.090
and accountable owners.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.360
Architecture connects those policies to systems.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.855
Identity platforms, endpoint controls, applications, networks, data services,

00:00:42.855 --> 00:00:47.115
and monitoring tools need consistent information and enforcement paths rather than

00:00:47.115 --> 00:00:49.035
isolated product decisions.

00:00:49.035 --> 00:00:51.100
Operational processes keep the model working.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.615
Access requests, employee changes, device enrollment, application onboarding,

00:00:54.615 --> 00:00:57.290
incident response, exception review, vendor access,

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.515
and decommissioning all affect whether Zero Trust remains accurate over time.

00:01:01.515 --> 00:01:03.265
People are part of the design.

00:01:03.265 --> 00:01:06.620
Employees and administrators need clear expectations, usable authentication,

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.490
understandable access requests, support when controls fail,

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:12.135
and training that explains why changes are occurring.

00:01:12.135 --> 00:01:16.495
Collaboration is essential because no single team owns every signal or enforcement point.

00:01:16.495 --> 00:01:20.680
Security, identity, network, endpoint, application, data, privacy, compliance,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.795
human resources, procurement, and business owners all contribute.

00:01:23.795 --> 00:01:26.190
A product purchase may improve one capability,

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.470
but it does not create Zero Trust by itself.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.935
The strategy succeeds when technology, policy, processes, ownership,

00:01:31.935 --> 00:01:35.390
and measurement produce consistent risk-informed access decisions.
